docs/research/2026-10-31-reference-context/role-v69b-draft.txt:4:- Read the item from its first paragraph to its last. Ask of each statement, in this order: (1) Is it new, or still in force, or a retelling the rows below allow? (2) Which question does it make more or less likely, and through which path? (3) Does one of the rows below reject it, and does that row's own boundary send it back to the first two questions? Then the analyst's own judgement: would you write it in this week's notes because of that change? Keep it when the answer is yes. For each kept item write the answers to (1) and (2) in new_in_it and moves, before the quote, each in a few words ("supplier doubles capacity by 2027"; "q4: more supply for the company"), never a sentence.
docs/research/2026-10-31-reference-context/role-v69b-draft.txt:5:- New (1): a new event or step, a new figure, a new forecast or plan, a new policy step, a new specific judgement ("the CFO said plants built a decade ago still run at full capacity"; "the regulator approved the drug for a second indication"; "the main supplier said its capacity will double by 2027"). Guidance, and a forecast or a plan still in force, count when retold; a retold past actual figure is optional; a development the item does not show to be old is not a retelling. Not new: an arrangement the item only describes as in place, with no new step, even when no date is given ("two airlines fly the planemaker's jets"; "the bank works with a payments firm on cards"); the company's history ("founded in 1998"); a claim everyone following the company makes without a figure and a specific account (that it leads, that demand is strong, that supply is tight: "rivals have spent billions but still lag"); a figure the item itself presents as already known, unless it is guidance or a forecast or plan still in force. new_in_it names what the item reports that the analyst did not have before; a restatement of the sentence is not an answer. Under the rows below, these are not it: the company's own account of what its product does or saves, with no business scale ("runs 40% more units in the same power"); an executive's or author's remark that a trend is strong or shifting, with no figure and no specific account ("compute is the new constraint"); a company named only as an example of who uses the product; a reporter's own market-size figure with no source.
docs/research/2026-10-31-reference-context/role-v69b-draft.txt:6:- Moves (2): name the question whose answer the statement makes more or less likely, whether it is open, worth opening, or too small to open, and say how ("a rival cut list prices 15% in Europe" lowers the company's pricing question; "the largest customer said it will cut orders by a fifth" lowers its revenue question). "Demand", "competitive position", "exposure", "opportunity" or "the ecosystem" on its own is not a question and "supports demand" does not say how: a statement you can connect to the company only in such words moves nothing and is not evidence. In links, name an open question's id when one fits, or your own candidate's id when the statement raises a new question; leave links empty when the question it moves is neither open nor worth opening (a customer's, a supplier's or a macro question), and write it in moves. Another company's action or figure needs such a path to a question about the company; a party next to the company only by sharing an article, an event or an ecosystem moves nothing. A statement that moves no question is not evidence, however specific.
docs/research/2026-10-31-reference-context/role-v69b-draft.txt:7:- Not kept (3), each row with its boundary: a product, game, driver or benchmark detail with no business scale, and a product comparison the company, a partner or a third party publishes ("the vacuum runs 60 minutes on a charge"; "the maker says its drug works twice as fast as older ones"); not a competitor's comparison against the company's product, nor a partner's or customer's new adoption step in the same sentence, nor the launch, shipment, delay or cancellation of the product that carries the company's value, nor the company's decision to change the price it charges: those go back to (1) and (2). A share-price move or an investor's position ("shares fell 13% this month"); the reaction after results is optional; the news behind a move goes back to (1) and (2). An unargued view, an author's general risk list or a quote that only characterises ("the stock is a hold"; "if demand slows it would hurt"; "a pivotal moment"), and a named analyst's reading of an event already recorded that adds no fact or calculation; a reading that adds one goes back to (1) and (2); a conditional valuation with its assumptions, derivation and date is kept. A political or strategic stance with no figure and no decision; an official's order, law or ruling is a policy step and goes back to (1) and (2). Another company's valuation, share price or deal that touches none of the company's markets; its actions and operating figures go back to (2). The same fact told again in other words (one deal, hire, programme or reported measure with its terms, figures and growth rates is one evidence item). Boilerplate, bylines, forward-looking-statement text, advertising and page material unrelated to the item.
